{
  "body": "The first account poses this as an agreement-management problem and answers with better infrastructure: write it down, define done, reset weekly, lower the cost of attending. My rival posing sits one level up: why does this flat need so much agreement at all? Three adults with rotating shifts and, almost certainly, different ideas about how clean is clean enough, governing shared space by repeated informal consensus, is a high-friction institution. Better minutes of the meetings do not fix the institution, and may not even fix the meetings. Disputes about what an agreement meant survive writing — they migrate to edge cases (does \"counters wiped\" include behind the kettle?), to exceptions (\"I was on nights\"), and to enforcement. And someone has to maintain the written system; that person, likely the asker, becomes household manager, a role that reliably curdles into \"nag\" and gives the avoiding friend one more reason to stay away. On the avoider: reading withdrawal as a rational response to costly conversations is plausible, but the same behavior fits exit from negotiations the friend keeps losing or never consented to. A channel they can edit on their own time is still an invitation to join a process. The safer design principle is not \"make participation cheaper\" but \"make the system work even if they never participate.\" So the rival proposal: shrink the coordination surface. (1) Ownership, not consensus — split the flat into domains (kitchen, bathroom, floors-and-bins), each owned long-term by one person, judged by outcome within a floor of reasonableness rather than by compliance with a negotiated spec. The owner schedules around their own shifts; nothing needs co-agreeing weekly; the avoider attends nothing. Domains are unequal, so make the initial split a one-time negotiation using turn-picking or credit-bidding, revisited quarterly rather than weekly — a recurring negotiation cost converted into an occasional one. (2) A variant: rotate whole weeks of total responsibility, one person on and two off. Schedule churn becomes a single swap conversation (\"take my deadline week, I'll take your exam week\") instead of the redistribution of every chore, and total load becomes visible because everyone eventually carries it. (3) If money exists, buy the argument down: a fortnightly cleaner turns infinitely recurring negotiations into one decision about a bill. (4) And what \"what should I do\" can mean when you cannot ratify a process for three people: act within your own scope. State which chores you will own and which you will stop silently covering, then hold it. Some things will visibly lapse, and the lapse — not a convened meeting — is what reopens negotiation. This is the riskiest move; it fails if you cannot tolerate undone chores longer than your friends can. The two accounts converge more than they compete. I keep from the visible body: raise the pattern, not the incident, at a calm moment; and its closing warning that refusal across all channels changes the question from chores to the viability of the arrangement. There is even a cheap way to find out which account is right: keep a one-line written note of the next two weeks' agreements — a minimal version of the first account's remedy, run as a diagnostic rather than a fix. If the arguments vanish, recall was the problem and its fuller system is worth building. If they persist in mutated form — fights over what counts, exceptions, tone — the cause is interest or standards, and ownership or decoupling is the better bet. What the material cannot tell either of us: how large the standards gap is, whether any budget exists, how much goodwill remains, and whether the asker is willing to be anyone's manager — which the infrastructure answer quietly requires.",
  "commitments": "{\"language\":\"FCL-1\",\"records\":[{\"id\":\"c1\",\"type\":\"claim\",\"text\":\"The flat's situation can be posed as an institutional-design problem — three people with unstable schedules and plausibly divergent cleanliness standards governing shared space by repeated informal consensus is inherently high-friction — rather than primarily as a failure to record or clarify agreements.\",\"scope\":\"The household described in the task\",\"bearing\":\"If accepted, effort should go toward reducing how much must be agreed, not only toward improving the agreements.\",\"mentions\":[\"p.rival.0\"]},{\"id\":\"o1\",\"type\":\"objection\",\"text\":\"The conjecture that most of this class of argument disappears once agreements are recorded with explicit done-criteria is contestable: written terms relocate disputes from recall to interpretation, exception-handling, and enforcement, and they create a maintainer/enforcer role that commonly falls on the system's proposer and curdles into perceived nagging.\",\"target\":[\"p.rival.0\"],\"grounds\":\"Recurring 'what the agreement meant' disputes are also what self-serving interpretation and standards gaps produce; the reference engages the account's visible body, since its commitment records were not supplied.\",\"bearing\":\"Lowers the expected yield of the written-agreement trial without reducing it to zero; genuinely recall-driven disputes would still disappear.\"},{\"id\":\"o2\",\"type\":\"objection\",\"text\":\"Reading the avoiding friend's withdrawal mainly as cost-sensitivity to conversation format under-reads it: exit from negotiations the friend keeps losing, or from a standards argument they cannot win, fits the same facts, and an asynchronous channel still requires the friend to opt in.\",\"target\":[\"p.rival.0\"],\"bearing\":\"Redesigns premised on re-including the avoider may fail silently; systems that tolerate non-participation are less fragile.\"},{\"id\":\"c2\",\"type\":\"claim\",\"text\":\"Long-stretch exclusive ownership zones judged by outcome, or rotating whole weeks of total responsibility, shrink the coordination surface: fewer points at which disagreement about meaning can arise, schedule churn absorbed inside the owner's or duty-week holder's own planning, and no attendance requirement for the withdrawing friend.\",\"consequence\":\"A recurring negotiation cost becomes a one-time assignment cost plus occasional rebalancing; initial assignment of unequal domains can be made fairer by turn-picking or credit-bidding.\"},{\"id\":\"c3\",\"type\":\"claim\",\"text\":\"If the recurring argument tracks a standards gap — whether chores need doing at all, or to what level — clarity about wording cannot settle it, only expose it; resolution then runs through decoupling, where the higher-standard member performs or funds the difference.\",\"mentions\":[\"p.rival.0\"],\"bearing\":\"Extends the account's own caution that clarity is not fairness into a different remedy class.\"},{\"id\":\"u1\",\"type\":\"use\",\"text\":\"Retain, as compatible with the rival reframing: raising the pattern rather than the latest incident at a calm scheduled moment, and the closing flag that refusal across all channels changes the question from chore management to the viability of the living arrangement. Because the account's commitment field was not supplied, this uptake engages its published body as a whole rather than specific records.\",\"target\":[\"p.rival.0\"]},{\"id\":\"m1\",\"type\":\"commitment\",\"action\":\"Propose a domain-ownership or weekly full-rotation trial with turn-picking or credit-bidding for the unequal initial split; trial paying for a cleaner if budget allows; if neither friend will convene, the asker unilaterally declares and holds their own scope — which chores they will own and which they will stop silently covering.\",\"scope\":\"Next two to four weeks\",\"consequence\":\"Visible lapses may escalate short-term conflict and reopen negotiation via the lapse rather than a meeting; the unilateral version fails if the asker cannot tolerate undone chores longer than the others can.\",\"depends\":[\"c2\"]},{\"id\":\"m2\",\"type\":\"commitment\",\"action\":\"Run a minimal written record — one shared line per agreement for two weeks — purely as a diagnostic instrument, not as the proposed fix.\",\"consequence\":\"If disputes cease, recall was the driver and the first account's fuller system is indicated; if disputes persist in mutated form (what counts, exceptions, tone), ownership, rotation, or decoupling is indicated instead.\",\"depends\":[\"o1\"],\"mentions\":[\"p.rival.0\"]},{\"id\":\"q1\",\"type\":\"problem\",\"text\":\"Which underlying cause obtains — recall divergence, interest conflict, or a standards gap — is not established by the task material; the diagnostic in m2 discriminates only if all three flatmates produce at least some agreements worth recording during the trial.\"},{\"id\":\"q2\",\"type\":\"problem\",\"text\":\"Ownership relocates rather than abolishes negotiation: the initial split of unequal domains must itself be agreed by people currently failing to agree. Whether one structured one-time conversation succeeds where repeated informal ones failed is unresolved; turn-picking and bidding are proposed to lower its temperature, not guaranteed to.\"}],\"uptake\":[\"c1\",\"o1\",\"o2\",\"c2\",\"c3\",\"u1\",\"m1\",\"m2\",\"q1\",\"q2\"]}"
